1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,600
In 1914,

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,200
people said the war would be over by Christmas

3
00:00:05,816 --> 00:00:06,926
Four years later,

4
00:00:06,926 --> 00:00:09,446
more than sixteen million people were dead

5
00:00:09,446 --> 00:00:11,326
This is the First World War,

6
00:00:11,326 --> 00:00:13,726
told through twelve true stories

7
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:23,800
After the shots in Sarajevo,

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:27,400
in just a few days in early August 1914,

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:30,160
the great powers of Europe declared war on one

10
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:31,280
another

11
00:00:33,228 --> 00:00:34,298
On one side,

12
00:00:34,298 --> 00:00:36,458
Germany and Austria-Hungary

13
00:00:36,458 --> 00:00:37,338
On the other,

14
00:00:37,338 --> 00:00:37,978
France,

15
00:00:37,978 --> 00:00:39,738
Russia and Britain

16
00:00:41,363 --> 00:00:44,113
Millions of young men rushed to enlist

17
00:00:44,113 --> 00:00:46,473
They believed the war would be short and

18
00:00:46,473 --> 00:00:47,593
glorious

19
00:00:49,704 --> 00:00:52,374
The German Kaiser is said to have promised his

20
00:00:52,374 --> 00:00:52,894
troops:

21
00:00:52,894 --> 00:00:55,494
you will be home before the leaves have fallen

22
00:00:55,494 --> 00:00:57,254
from the trees

23
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:03,080
Germany's plan was bold:

24
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:05,480
strike fast through neutral Belgium,

25
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,520
then swing south and take Paris in about six

26
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:09,640
weeks

27
00:01:11,464 --> 00:01:13,094
But Belgium fought back

28
00:01:13,094 --> 00:01:15,854
And the invasion of Belgium brought Britain

29
00:01:15,854 --> 00:01:18,254
into the war against Germany

30
00:01:20,129 --> 00:01:21,279
By early September,

31
00:01:21,279 --> 00:01:24,079
the Germans were only about fifty kilometres

32
00:01:24,079 --> 00:01:25,519
from Paris

33
00:01:32,983 --> 00:01:34,693
September 1914

34
00:01:34,693 --> 00:01:36,173
Paris was in a panic

35
00:01:36,173 --> 00:01:38,453
The French government had already fled the

36
00:01:38,453 --> 00:01:39,573
capital

37
00:01:41,474 --> 00:01:43,824
French and British troops launched a

38
00:01:43,824 --> 00:01:46,224
counterattack along the River Marne

39
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:49,951
On the night of the sixth of September,

40
00:01:49,951 --> 00:01:52,831
the army requisitioned the taxis of Paris to

41
00:01:52,831 --> 00:01:55,231
carry soldiers to the front

42
00:01:57,049 --> 00:01:59,759
Hundreds of taxis drove in convoy through the

43
00:01:59,759 --> 00:02:00,199
night

44
00:02:00,199 --> 00:02:01,799
The meters kept running,

45
00:02:01,799 --> 00:02:04,399
and the army later paid the fares

46
00:02:07,662 --> 00:02:10,262
They carried about five thousand men

47
00:02:12,114 --> 00:02:12,903
In truth,

48
00:02:12,903 --> 00:02:15,064
that was tiny in a battle of more than

49
00:02:15,064 --> 00:02:16,623
a million soldiers

50
00:02:16,623 --> 00:02:19,023
Most reinforcements arrived by train

51
00:02:21,976 --> 00:02:23,546
But the Germans were stopped

52
00:02:23,546 --> 00:02:24,766
Paris was saved,

53
00:02:24,766 --> 00:02:27,366
and the taxis became a legend

54
00:02:29,492 --> 00:02:31,962
Germany's plan for a quick victory had

55
00:02:31,962 --> 00:02:32,882
collapsed

56
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:35,482
Both sides began to dig into the ground

57
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:45,800
By the end of 1914,

58
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,280
a line of trenches stretched more than seven

59
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:49,720
hundred kilometres,

60
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:52,320
from the North Sea to the Swiss border

61
00:02:54,496 --> 00:02:57,046
Between the two lines lay No Man's Land,

62
00:02:57,046 --> 00:02:59,646
full of barbed wire and shell craters

63
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:03,271
Soldiers lived in mud,

64
00:03:03,271 --> 00:03:05,431
standing water and bitter cold

65
00:03:05,431 --> 00:03:08,431
Rats were so common they even ate the soldiers'

66
00:03:08,431 --> 00:03:09,551
rations

67
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:14,720
After days standing in cold water,

68
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:16,640
many men got trench foot

69
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:19,240
Their feet swelled up and began to rot

70
00:03:21,409 --> 00:03:22,439
In every attack,

71
00:03:22,439 --> 00:03:25,079
soldiers had to climb out of the trench and

72
00:03:25,079 --> 00:03:27,159
charge into machine-gun fire

73
00:03:29,765 --> 00:03:31,875
Sometimes thousands of men fell,

74
00:03:31,875 --> 00:03:34,475
just to gain a few hundred metres

75
00:03:36,647 --> 00:03:37,957
For more than three years,

76
00:03:37,957 --> 00:03:40,357
the Western Front barely moved

77
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:50,433
On Christmas Eve 1914,

78
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:51,673
on the Western Front,

79
00:03:51,673 --> 00:03:54,793
German soldiers lit candles on small Christmas

80
00:03:54,793 --> 00:03:55,913
trees

81
00:03:57,996 --> 00:04:00,426
Then they began to sing Silent Night

82
00:04:00,426 --> 00:04:02,026
From the trenches opposite,

83
00:04:02,026 --> 00:04:04,106
British soldiers sang back

84
00:04:06,495 --> 00:04:07,465
The next morning,

85
00:04:07,465 --> 00:04:10,065
unarmed soldiers walked out into No Man's Land

86
00:04:13,091 --> 00:04:14,161
They shook hands,

87
00:04:14,161 --> 00:04:16,041
swapped cigarettes and sweets,

88
00:04:16,041 --> 00:04:18,441
and buried their dead together

89
00:04:21,898 --> 00:04:23,088
In a few places,

90
00:04:23,088 --> 00:04:25,688
they even played football on the frozen ground

91
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:31,137
Some estimates say up to one hundred thousand

92
00:04:31,137 --> 00:04:33,737
men took part in these unofficial truces

93
00:04:36,399 --> 00:04:37,869
The commanders were furious,

94
00:04:37,869 --> 00:04:40,469
and ordered that it must never happen again

95
00:04:42,513 --> 00:04:45,113
It was the only Christmas truce of the war

96
00:04:53,413 --> 00:04:55,933
The afternoon of the twenty-second of April

97
00:04:55,933 --> 00:04:57,093
1915,

98
00:04:57,093 --> 00:04:59,693
near the Belgian town of Ypres

99
00:05:01,331 --> 00:05:02,441
At five o'clock,

100
00:05:02,441 --> 00:05:05,481
German troops opened the valves on thousands of

101
00:05:05,481 --> 00:05:06,681
gas cylinders

102
00:05:06,681 --> 00:05:09,621
About one hundred and sixty-eight tonnes of

103
00:05:09,621 --> 00:05:11,381
chlorine poured out

104
00:05:13,468 --> 00:05:16,097
A yellow-green cloud drifted on the wind,

105
00:05:16,097 --> 00:05:18,497
straight into the French trenches

106
00:05:23,088 --> 00:05:24,758
No one had a gas mask

107
00:05:24,758 --> 00:05:25,878
Soldiers choked,

108
00:05:25,878 --> 00:05:27,958
and fled in panic

109
00:05:29,652 --> 00:05:32,522
A gap several kilometres wide opened in the

110
00:05:32,522 --> 00:05:33,642
line

111
00:05:35,105 --> 00:05:37,295
Chemical weapons had entered the war

112
00:05:37,295 --> 00:05:37,655
Soon,

113
00:05:37,655 --> 00:05:40,255
both sides were using poison gas

114
00:05:42,711 --> 00:05:45,221
The gas mask became the haunting image of the

115
00:05:45,221 --> 00:05:46,981
First World War

116
00:05:54,917 --> 00:05:57,267
On the seventh of May 1915,

117
00:05:57,267 --> 00:06:00,147
the British liner Lusitania was sailing off the

118
00:06:00,147 --> 00:06:01,907
coast of Ireland

119
00:06:03,684 --> 00:06:06,434
On board were nearly two thousand passengers

120
00:06:06,434 --> 00:06:07,874
and crew

121
00:06:09,103 --> 00:06:12,155
The German submarine U-20 fired a single

122
00:06:12,155 --> 00:06:13,275
torpedo

123
00:06:14,936 --> 00:06:16,526
In just eighteen minutes,

124
00:06:16,526 --> 00:06:18,926
the giant ship was gone

125
00:06:23,856 --> 00:06:26,126
Around twelve hundred people died,

126
00:06:26,126 --> 00:06:28,646
including one hundred and twenty-eight

127
00:06:28,646 --> 00:06:29,766
Americans

128
00:06:32,141 --> 00:06:33,811
America was outraged

129
00:06:33,811 --> 00:06:36,411
But it still stayed out of the war

130
00:06:38,182 --> 00:06:40,572
It would take almost two more years,

131
00:06:40,572 --> 00:06:42,212
and a secret telegram,

132
00:06:42,212 --> 00:06:44,292
to drag America in

133
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:54,540
On the morning of the twenty-first of February

134
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:55,660
1916,

135
00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:58,060
the German army attacked Verdun

136
00:07:00,209 --> 00:07:02,839
German general Falkenhayn wanted to bleed the

137
00:07:02,839 --> 00:07:04,119
French army white,

138
00:07:04,119 --> 00:07:06,519
down to the last man

139
00:07:07,837 --> 00:07:09,827
Millions of shells rained down

140
00:07:09,827 --> 00:07:11,547
Whole forests were flattened,

141
00:07:11,547 --> 00:07:14,147
and villages vanished from the map

142
00:07:16,169 --> 00:07:18,199
The French rallied to a famous cry:

143
00:07:18,199 --> 00:07:20,279
they shall not pass!

144
00:07:22,129 --> 00:07:23,038
Day and night,

145
00:07:23,038 --> 00:07:26,098
trucks rolled along the only supply road into

146
00:07:26,098 --> 00:07:26,519
Verdun

147
00:07:26,519 --> 00:07:29,119
The French called it the Sacred Way

148
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:33,330
The battle lasted about ten months,

149
00:07:33,330 --> 00:07:35,090
until December 1916

150
00:07:37,820 --> 00:07:40,650
More than seven hundred thousand casualties

151
00:07:40,650 --> 00:07:41,730
And in the end,

152
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:44,250
the front line was almost back where it

153
00:07:44,250 --> 00:07:45,370
started

154
00:07:53,317 --> 00:07:55,747
On the first of July 1916,

155
00:07:55,747 --> 00:07:58,307
British and French troops launched a huge

156
00:07:58,307 --> 00:08:00,707
attack along the River Somme

157
00:08:02,204 --> 00:08:03,714
For a whole week beforehand,

158
00:08:03,714 --> 00:08:05,674
British guns fired nonstop

159
00:08:05,674 --> 00:08:08,274
They believed no German soldier could survive

160
00:08:10,831 --> 00:08:12,941
But the Germans were sheltering in deep

161
00:08:12,941 --> 00:08:13,781
dugouts

162
00:08:13,781 --> 00:08:15,181
When the shelling stopped,

163
00:08:15,181 --> 00:08:17,581
the machine guns opened fire

164
00:08:19,497 --> 00:08:20,967
On the first day alone,

165
00:08:20,967 --> 00:08:23,327
the British suffered more than fifty-seven

166
00:08:23,327 --> 00:08:24,847
thousand casualties

167
00:08:24,847 --> 00:08:27,247
It was the darkest day in the history of

168
00:08:27,247 --> 00:08:29,007
the British Army

169
00:08:31,125 --> 00:08:32,915
On the fifteenth of September,

170
00:08:32,915 --> 00:08:35,115
the British unveiled a new weapon:

171
00:08:35,115 --> 00:08:36,555
the tank

172
00:08:38,265 --> 00:08:39,415
To keep it secret,

173
00:08:39,415 --> 00:08:41,655
workers were told they were building water

174
00:08:41,655 --> 00:08:42,375
tanks

175
00:08:42,375 --> 00:08:44,975
And the name tank has stuck ever since

176
00:08:47,261 --> 00:08:49,211
About forty-nine were made ready,

177
00:08:49,211 --> 00:08:51,531
but many broke down or got stuck before the

178
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,291
attack even began

179
00:08:55,124 --> 00:08:55,874
Even so,

180
00:08:55,874 --> 00:08:58,834
German soldiers were terrified to see steel

181
00:08:58,834 --> 00:09:01,394
machines crawling over their trenches

182
00:09:01,394 --> 00:09:03,474
War had changed forever

183
00:09:11,817 --> 00:09:13,847
In January 1917,

184
00:09:13,847 --> 00:09:16,407
German foreign minister Arthur Zimmermann sent

185
00:09:16,407 --> 00:09:18,807
a secret telegram to Mexico

186
00:09:21,028 --> 00:09:21,658
The message:

187
00:09:21,658 --> 00:09:23,418
if America entered the war,

188
00:09:23,418 --> 00:09:25,778
Germany offered Mexico an alliance,

189
00:09:25,778 --> 00:09:27,858
and help to win back Texas,

190
00:09:27,858 --> 00:09:29,938
New Mexico and Arizona

191
00:09:32,458 --> 00:09:34,808
But the British intercepted the telegram

192
00:09:34,808 --> 00:09:36,168
In Room Forty,

193
00:09:36,168 --> 00:09:37,928
codebreakers cracked it

194
00:09:40,045 --> 00:09:41,315
On the first of March,

195
00:09:41,315 --> 00:09:44,435
the telegram appeared in American newspapers

196
00:09:44,435 --> 00:09:47,195
Many thought it was fake news invented by

197
00:09:47,195 --> 00:09:48,315
Britain

198
00:09:50,066 --> 00:09:52,256
Then Zimmermann himself admitted it:

199
00:09:52,256 --> 00:09:52,816
yes,

200
00:09:52,816 --> 00:09:54,576
I sent it

201
00:09:56,336 --> 00:09:57,366
At the same time,

202
00:09:57,366 --> 00:09:59,926
German submarines were once again sinking

203
00:09:59,926 --> 00:10:02,006
merchant ships without warning

204
00:10:03,923 --> 00:10:06,593
On the sixth of April 1917,

205
00:10:06,593 --> 00:10:09,193
the United States declared war on Germany

206
00:10:17,883 --> 00:10:19,393
October 1918

207
00:10:19,393 --> 00:10:21,633
In the Argonne Forest in France,

208
00:10:21,633 --> 00:10:24,513
more than five hundred American soldiers were

209
00:10:24,513 --> 00:10:26,593
surrounded by German troops

210
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:31,650
They became known as the Lost Battalion

211
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:33,969
Worse still,

212
00:10:33,969 --> 00:10:36,769
American artillery didn't know where they were,

213
00:10:36,769 --> 00:10:39,369
and began shelling their own men

214
00:10:41,225 --> 00:10:42,095
Their commander,

215
00:10:42,095 --> 00:10:43,295
Charles Whittlesey,

216
00:10:43,295 --> 00:10:44,855
released his last pigeon,

217
00:10:44,855 --> 00:10:46,175
named Cher Ami,

218
00:10:46,175 --> 00:10:47,135
with a message:

219
00:10:47,135 --> 00:10:50,015
our own artillery is shelling us directly

220
00:10:50,015 --> 00:10:51,175
For heaven's sake,

221
00:10:51,175 --> 00:10:52,615
stop it

222
00:10:54,723 --> 00:10:56,633
The bird was shot through the breast,

223
00:10:56,633 --> 00:10:57,953
blinded in one eye,

224
00:10:57,953 --> 00:11:00,353
and nearly lost a leg

225
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,590
But it still flew back to its loft,

226
00:11:06,590 --> 00:11:08,670
about forty kilometres away

227
00:11:11,019 --> 00:11:12,210
The shelling stopped

228
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:14,810
One hundred and ninety-four men were saved

229
00:11:17,629 --> 00:11:20,536
France awarded Cher Ami the Croix de Guerre

230
00:11:20,536 --> 00:11:20,909
Today,

231
00:11:20,909 --> 00:11:23,149
the bird is still on display at the

232
00:11:23,149 --> 00:11:24,269
Smithsonian

233
00:11:32,517 --> 00:11:33,707
In 1918,

234
00:11:33,707 --> 00:11:35,467
as the war neared its end,

235
00:11:35,467 --> 00:11:37,467
an invisible enemy appeared:

236
00:11:37,467 --> 00:11:38,587
influenza

237
00:11:40,903 --> 00:11:43,093
Soldiers were crammed into camps,

238
00:11:43,093 --> 00:11:44,613
ships and trenches

239
00:11:44,613 --> 00:11:47,213
The virus travelled with them around the world

240
00:11:49,873 --> 00:11:52,303
About five hundred million people were

241
00:11:52,303 --> 00:11:52,923
infected,

242
00:11:52,923 --> 00:11:55,523
nearly a third of the world's population

243
00:11:58,016 --> 00:11:58,686
Strangely,

244
00:11:58,686 --> 00:12:01,326
it killed the young and healthy most of all,

245
00:12:01,326 --> 00:12:03,726
people aged twenty to thirty

246
00:12:05,735 --> 00:12:07,685
It was called the Spanish flu

247
00:12:07,685 --> 00:12:10,285
But it probably didn't start there

248
00:12:12,296 --> 00:12:14,926
Spain was neutral and its newspapers weren't

249
00:12:14,926 --> 00:12:15,366
censored,

250
00:12:15,366 --> 00:12:18,326
so it reported on the outbreak more than anyone

251
00:12:18,326 --> 00:12:19,446
else

252
00:12:20,962 --> 00:12:23,712
At least fifty million people died of the flu,

253
00:12:23,712 --> 00:12:26,312
more than were killed by the war itself

254
00:12:34,617 --> 00:12:37,527
In the early hours of the eleventh of November

255
00:12:37,527 --> 00:12:38,687
1918,

256
00:12:38,687 --> 00:12:41,167
in a railway carriage in the forest of

257
00:12:41,167 --> 00:12:41,887
Compiègne,

258
00:12:41,887 --> 00:12:43,967
Germany signed the armistice

259
00:12:46,026 --> 00:12:48,536
The ceasefire would begin at eleven o'clock

260
00:12:48,536 --> 00:12:49,776
The eleventh hour,

261
00:12:49,776 --> 00:12:51,096
of the eleventh day,

262
00:12:51,096 --> 00:12:53,176
of the eleventh month

263
00:12:54,963 --> 00:12:57,153
But during those six hours of waiting,

264
00:12:57,153 --> 00:12:59,233
the fighting went on

265
00:13:00,755 --> 00:13:03,425
Nearly three thousand soldiers were killed on

266
00:13:03,425 --> 00:13:06,025
the last day of the war

267
00:13:07,622 --> 00:13:08,852
At ten fifty-nine,

268
00:13:08,852 --> 00:13:10,892
American private Henry Gunther,

269
00:13:10,892 --> 00:13:12,532
twenty-three years old,

270
00:13:12,532 --> 00:13:15,452
charged at a German machine-gun post and was

271
00:13:15,452 --> 00:13:16,572
shot

272
00:13:18,398 --> 00:13:19,508
One minute later,

273
00:13:19,508 --> 00:13:21,908
the whole front fell silent

274
00:13:25,673 --> 00:13:28,183
People cheered and embraced in the streets

275
00:13:28,183 --> 00:13:28,863
everywhere

276
00:13:28,863 --> 00:13:31,463
They called it the war to end all wars

277
00:13:36,583 --> 00:13:39,453
On the twenty-eighth of June 1919,

278
00:13:39,453 --> 00:13:43,053
exactly five years after the shots in Sarajevo,

279
00:13:43,053 --> 00:13:45,413
the Treaty of Versailles was signed

280
00:13:45,413 --> 00:13:48,293
Germany was forced to accept the blame and pay

281
00:13:48,293 --> 00:13:49,733
enormous reparations

282
00:13:51,896 --> 00:13:53,806
Four great empires collapsed:

283
00:13:53,806 --> 00:13:54,566
the German,

284
00:13:54,566 --> 00:13:56,046
the Austro-Hungarian,

285
00:13:56,046 --> 00:13:57,686
the Russian and the Ottoman

286
00:13:57,686 --> 00:14:00,046
The map of Europe and the Middle East was

287
00:14:00,046 --> 00:14:01,166
redrawn

288
00:14:02,671 --> 00:14:03,101
Today,

289
00:14:03,101 --> 00:14:04,541
in many countries,

290
00:14:04,541 --> 00:14:07,341
people still fall silent at eleven o'clock on

291
00:14:07,341 --> 00:14:08,901
